\begin{abstract}
Pneumonia remains a leading cause of mortality worldwide, necessitating rapid and accurate diagnosis for effective treatment. This paper presents a comprehensive comparative study of deep learning architectures for automated pneumonia detection from chest X-ray images. We implemented and evaluated five distinct approaches: a custom Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) without augmentation as baseline, CNN with medical-specific data augmentation, ResNet18 with transfer learning, DenseNet121 with dense connections, and a Supervised Contrastive Learning (SupCon) approach with two-stage training. Our experiments were conducted on a dataset of 5,832 chest X-ray images from the Kaggle Pneumonia Dataset. Results demonstrate that DenseNet121 achieved superior performance with 89.10\% accuracy, 88.76\% weighted F1-score, and 0.7702 Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC), followed by CNN with augmentation (86.06\% accuracy), SupCon (85.58\% accuracy), and ResNet18 (85.90\% accuracy). The SupCon approach demonstrated exceptional pneumonia sensitivity (99\% recall) with only 2 false negatives, making it particularly suitable for screening applications where missing pneumonia cases carries critical risk. The baseline CNN without augmentation achieved only 76.60\% accuracy, highlighting the critical importance of data augmentation in medical imaging applications. Data augmentation improved CNN performance by 9.46\%, reducing false positives from 143 to 71 cases. All models demonstrated high pneumonia sensitivity (96-99\%), crucial for clinical screening applications. This study provides practical insights for deploying automated pneumonia detection systems in healthcare settings and establishes benchmarks for future research in medical image analysis.
\end{abstract}

