\subsubsection{ResNet18 Implementation}

ResNet18 leverages residual connections to enable training of deeper networks while avoiding vanishing gradient problems. We adapted the architecture for binary pneumonia classification.


\textbf{Architecture Modifications:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Utilizes ImageNet pre-trained weights for transfer learning
\item Modified final classification layer for binary output
\item Input converted to 3-channel RGB for compatibility
\item Residual connections preserve gradient flow
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Transfer Learning Benefits:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Pre-trained features from ImageNet provide robust low-level representations
\item Faster convergence compared to training from scratch
\item Improved generalization on limited medical data
\item Proven architecture reliability
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{DenseNet121 Implementation}

DenseNet121 \cite{huang2017densely} implements dense connectivity patterns where each layer receives feature maps from all preceding layers, promoting feature reuse and parameter efficiency.

\textbf{Dense Connectivity Advantages:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Maximum information flow between layers
\item Feature reuse reduces parameter redundancy
\item Gradient flow improvement through skip connections
\item Enhanced feature propagation
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Architecture Benefits:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Dense connections concatenate features from all previous layers
\item Efficient parameter usage through feature reuse
\item Strong gradient flow prevents vanishing gradients
\item Excellent performance on limited training data
\end{itemize}

