\section{Supervised Contrastive Learning Implementation}

We implemented a fifth approach using Supervised Contrastive Learning (SupCon) \cite{khosla2020supervised}, which leverages label information to learn better feature representations by pulling together samples of the same class while pushing apart samples from different classes in the learned embedding space.

\subsubsection{SupCon Architecture}

The SupCon model consists of three main components: a backbone encoder, a projection head for contrastive learning, and a classification head for final predictions.

\textbf{Backbone Encoder:} We utilized ResNet18 pre-trained on ImageNet as the feature extraction backbone. The final fully connected layer is replaced with an identity function to extract feature representations:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python, caption=SupCon Backbone Configuration]
backbone_model = models.resnet18(weights="IMAGENET1K_V1")
dim_in = backbone_model.fc.in_features  // 512 features
backbone_model.fc = nn.Identity()
self.encoder = backbone_model
\end{lstlisting}

\textbf{Projection Head:} A non-linear projection head maps the backbone features to a lower-dimensional space optimized for contrastive learning:

\begin{equation}
h = f_{\theta}(x), \quad z = g_{\phi}(h)
\end{equation}

where $f_{\theta}$ is the backbone encoder and $g_{\phi}$ is the projection head implemented as:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python, caption=Projection Head Architecture]
self.projection_head = nn.Sequential(
    nn.Linear(dim_in, dim_in // 2),      // 512 -> 256
    nn.BatchNorm1d(dim_in // 2),
    nn.ReLU(inplace=True),
    nn.Dropout(dropout_rate),
    nn.Linear(dim_in // 2, feat_dim),    // 256 -> 128
)
\end{lstlisting}

\textbf{Classification Head:} A separate head performs the final binary classification:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python, caption=Classification Head Architecture]
self.classification_head = nn.Sequential(
    nn.Dropout(dropout_rate),
    nn.Linear(feat_dim, feat_dim // 2),  // 128 -> 64
    nn.ReLU(inplace=True),
    nn.Dropout(dropout_rate * 0.5),
    nn.Linear(feat_dim // 2, num_classes), // 64 -> 1
)
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection{Supervised Contrastive Loss Function}

The SupCon loss function maximizes agreement between augmented versions of samples from the same class while minimizing agreement with samples from different classes. For a given anchor sample $i$ with label $y_i$, the loss is defined as:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{SupCon} = \sum_{i \in I} \mathcal{L}_{SupCon}^{(i)}
\end{equation}

where the loss for sample $i$ is:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{SupCon}^{(i)} = -\frac{1}{|P(i)|} \sum_{p \in P(i)} \log \frac{\exp(z_i \cdot z_p / \tau)}{\sum_{a \in A(i)} \exp(z_i \cdot z_a / \tau)}
\end{equation}

where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $P(i) = \{p \in A(i) : y_p = y_i\}$ is the set of positive samples (same class)
\item $A(i) = I \setminus \{i\}$ is the set of all other samples in the batch
\item $\tau$ is the temperature parameter controlling the concentration of the distribution
\item $z_i$ and $z_p$ are L2-normalized projected features
\end{itemize}

The key insight is that $P(i)$ includes all samples with the same label as the anchor, not just augmented versions of the same image, enabling the model to learn class-discriminative features.

\subsubsection{Two-Stage Training Protocol}

We employed a two-stage training protocol to optimize the SupCon model effectively:

\textbf{Stage 1 - Contrastive Representation Learning :}
During this stage, we train only with the contrastive loss to learn robust feature representations. For each batch, we create two augmented views of each image using medical imaging-appropriate transformations:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python, caption=Strong Augmentation for Contrastive Learning]
strong_aug = transforms.Compose([
    transforms.RandomAffine(degrees=15, translate=(0.1, 0.1), 
                          scale=(0.9, 1.1)),
    transforms.RandomHorizontalFlip(p=0.5),
    transforms.ColorJitter(brightness=0.3, contrast=0.3, 
                          saturation=0.2),
    transforms.RandomAdjustSharpness(1.5, p=0.3),
    transforms.GaussianBlur(kernel_size=3, sigma=(0.1, 1.0))
])
\end{lstlisting}

The contrastive features are computed and normalized:
\begin{equation}
z_1 = \frac{g_{\phi}(f_{\theta}(T_1(x)))}{||g_{\phi}(f_{\theta}(T_1(x)))||_2}, \quad z_2 = \frac{g_{\phi}(f_{\theta}(T_2(x)))}{||g_{\phi}(f_{\theta}(T_2(x)))||_2}
\end{equation}

where $T_1$ and $T_2$ are different augmentation transformations.

\textbf{Stage 2 - Classification Fine-tuning :}
In the second stage, we freeze the learned representations and train the classification head using binary cross-entropy loss with class balancing:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{BCE} = -\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i [y_i \log(\sigma(h_{\psi}(z_i))) + (1-y_i) \log(1-\sigma(h_{\psi}(z_i)))]
\end{equation}

where $h_{\psi}$ is the classification head, $\sigma$ is the sigmoid function, and $w_i$ are class weights to handle imbalance.

