\subsubsection{SupCon Performance Analysis}

The SupCon approach demonstrates several notable characteristics:

\textbf{Strengths:}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{High Pneumonia Sensitivity:} Achieved 99\% recall for pneumonia detection, the highest among all models, making it excellent for screening applications where missing pneumonia cases is critical.
\item \textbf{Exceptional Specificity:} With only 2 false negatives, the model rarely misses actual pneumonia cases, crucial for patient safety.
\item \textbf{Robust Feature Learning:} The two-stage training protocol enables learning of discriminative features that generalize well to the classification task.
\item \textbf{Competitive Overall Performance:} Achieved 85.58\% accuracy, positioning it between the augmented CNN and transfer learning approaches.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Limitations:}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{High False Positive Rate:} 88 false positives indicate the model tends to over-diagnose pneumonia in normal cases, potentially increasing radiologist workload.
\item \textbf{Training Complexity:} The two-stage training protocol requires careful hyperparameter tuning and longer training time compared to single-stage approaches.
\item \textbf{Lower Normal Class Precision:} While achieving high pneumonia detection, the model struggles with normal case identification (62\% recall for normal cases).
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Clinical Implications:}
The SupCon model's characteristics make it particularly suitable for initial screening applications where the primary goal is to avoid missing pneumonia cases. The 99\% pneumonia sensitivity ensures that almost all pneumonia cases are flagged for radiologist review, while the 2 false negatives represent an acceptably low risk of missed diagnoses. However, the high false positive rate (88 cases) may require careful integration into clinical workflows to manage radiologist workload effectively.

\textbf{Comparison with Other Approaches:}
While SupCon did not achieve the highest overall accuracy compared to DenseNet121 (89.10\%), it demonstrated unique advantages in terms of pneumonia detection sensitivity. The contrastive learning approach successfully learned representations that excel at identifying pneumonia patterns, even if they occasionally misclassify normal cases as pneumonia. This conservative approach aligns well with medical screening requirements where sensitivity is often prioritized over specificity.


\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Clinical Significance}

From a clinical perspective, the results demonstrate varying strengths of different approaches:

\textbf{DenseNet121} emerges as the optimal choice for balanced screening applications, achieving 89.10\% accuracy with excellent precision-recall balance. The model's 98\% pneumonia sensitivity ensures minimal missed diagnoses, while 74\% normal recall reduces false alarms.

\textbf{SupCon} demonstrates exceptional clinical utility for high-sensitivity screening scenarios, achieving the highest pneumonia sensitivity (99\%) with only 2 false negatives across the entire test set. This ultra-conservative approach makes it ideal for initial screening applications where missing a pneumonia diagnosis carries severe consequences, though the 88 false positives require careful workflow integration to manage radiologist review burden.

\textbf{ResNet18} shows competitive performance (85.90\% accuracy) with 97\% pneumonia sensitivity, making it suitable for high-sensitivity screening scenarios where missing pneumonia cases is critically unacceptable.

\textbf{CNN with Augmentation} provides acceptable performance (86.06\% accuracy) with lower computational requirements, suitable for resource-constrained environments.

\subsection{Technical Insights}

\subsubsection{Architecture Comparison}

The superior performance of DenseNet121 can be attributed to its dense connectivity pattern, which promotes feature reuse and gradient flow. This architecture is particularly beneficial for medical imaging where subtle features may be crucial for accurate diagnosis.

The SupCon approach's effectiveness stems from its two-stage training protocol that first learns discriminative feature representations through contrastive learning before fine-tuning for classification. The contrastive learning stage enables the model to learn robust features that distinguish between normal and pneumonia cases by maximizing agreement between augmented views of the same class while pushing apart different classes in the embedding space. This representation learning approach proves particularly effective for medical imaging where subtle pathological patterns require sophisticated feature extraction.

ResNet18's performance demonstrates the effectiveness of residual connections in medical image analysis, while the custom CNN's improvement with augmentation validates the importance of domain-specific data preprocessing.

\subsubsection{Transfer Learning Effectiveness}

Both ResNet18 and DenseNet121 benefited from ImageNet pre-training, despite the domain gap between natural and medical images. This suggests that low-level features learned on natural images are transferable to medical imaging applications.

\subsection{Data Augmentation Analysis}

The substantial improvement achieved through data augmentation (+9.46\% accuracy) highlights several key benefits:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Increased Data Diversity:} Augmentation effectively increases training data variety without additional annotation costs
\item \textbf{Improved Generalization:} Models become more robust to variations in image acquisition parameters
\item \textbf{Class Balance Enhancement:} Augmentation helps address the inherent class imbalance in medical datasets
\item \textbf{Overfitting Reduction:} Prevents memorization of specific image characteristics
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Limitations and Challenges}

Several limitations should be acknowledged:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Dataset Scope:} Single-institution data may limit generalizability
\item \textbf{Binary Classification:} Current approach does not distinguish between pneumonia types (viral vs bacterial)
\item \textbf{Localization:} Models do not provide spatial localization of pathological regions
\item \textbf{External Validation:} Performance on external datasets requires further investigation
\end{itemize}

