\section{Conclusion}

This comprehensive study evaluated four deep learning approaches for automated pneumonia detection from chest X-ray images. Key findings include:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{DenseNet121 Superior Performance:} Achieved highest accuracy (89.10\%) and best clinical metrics, establishing it as the recommended architecture for balanced pneumonia detection systems.

\item \textbf{Critical Role of Data Augmentation:} Medical imaging-specific augmentation improved CNN performance by 9.46\%, transforming an unreliable baseline into a clinically viable system.

\item \textbf{Transfer Learning Effectiveness:} Pre-trained models consistently outperformed custom architectures, demonstrating the value of ImageNet features for medical imaging.

\item \textbf{Clinical Viability:} All advanced models achieved pneumonia sensitivity >95\%, meeting requirements for clinical screening applications.
\end{enumerate}

The study establishes practical benchmarks for pneumonia detection research and provides implementation guidelines for clinical deployment. Our open-source implementation facilitates reproducibility and further research in automated medical image analysis.

For clinical deployment, we recommend DenseNet121 for balanced screening applications, ResNet18 for high-sensitivity scenarios, and augmented CNN for resource-constrained environments. Future work should focus on external validation, multi-class classification, and explainable AI integration to enhance clinical adoption and trust.

